The 401 on /stream occurs because the browser's native EventSource API does not support custom headers. It will always strip the Authorization: Bearer token, causing bearer-guarded endpoints to reject the connection.

Since you are operating within Backstage (where fetchApiRef automatically injects the identity token), you must replace new EventSource with a stream-aware fetch implementation. Here is the surgical fix.

Option 1: The Standard Drop-In (Recommended)
The industry standard solution for this exact limitation is @microsoft/fetch-event-source. It mimics the EventSource API but sits on top of fetch, allowing headers to pass through seamlessly.

Install the package in the app package:
yarn workspace app add @microsoft/fetch-event-source

Update the three call sites (FleetReactorApp.tsx, Fleet.tsx, Board.tsx):

TypeScript
import { fetchEventSource } from '@microsoft/fetch-event-source';
import { useApi, fetchApiRef } from '@backstage/core-plugin-api';

// Inside your component:
const { fetch } = useApi(fetchApiRef); // Backstage's authenticated fetch

useEffect(() => {
  const abortController = new AbortController();

  fetchEventSource(`${FLEETVIEW}/stream`, {
    fetch: fetch, // Injects the Bearer token automatically
    signal: abortController.signal,
    onmessage(ev) {
      // Handle your SSE data here
      console.log('Event received:', ev.data);
    },
    onerror(err) {
      console.error('SSE Error:', err);
    }
  });

  return () => abortController.abort(); // Cleanup replaces sse.close()
}, [fetch]);
Option 2: The Zero-Dependency Fallback
If adding a dependency will stall your CI or require approvals, implement this lightweight, zero-dependency manual reader using Backstage's authenticated fetch.

TypeScript
import { useApi, fetchApiRef } from '@backstage/core-plugin-api';

// Inside your component:
const { fetch } = useApi(fetchApiRef);

useEffect(() => {
  const abortController = new AbortController();

  const connectStream = async () => {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(`${FLEETVIEW}/stream`, { 
        signal: abortController.signal 
      });

      if (!response.body) return;

      const reader = response.body.getReader();
      const decoder = new TextDecoder();

      while (true) {
        const { value, done } = await reader.read();
        if (done) break;

        const chunk = decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
        // Minimal SSE parser: split by newlines and extract data
        const lines = chunk.split('\n');
        for (const line of lines) {
          if (line.startsWith('data: ')) {
            const data = line.slice(6).trim();
            // Handle your SSE data here
            console.log('Event received:', data);
          }
        }
      }
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') {
        console.error('Stream failed:', err);
      }
    }
  };

  connectStream();

  return () => abortController.abort();
}, [fetch]);
Why this works immediately: Both approaches route the stream request through Backstage's fetchApiRef, inheriting the same token injection that allows /efficiency and /voice/voices to return 200s, permanently bypassing the native EventSource header limitation.

